wables by a shader / transparency etc. It's also possible to have multiple =
cameras and switch among them.</p><p>Besides drawables, there are other fea=
tures for animation, audio, physics, etc.</p><div class=3D"m-clearfix-m"></=
div></section><section id=3D"scenegraph-transformation"><h2><a href=3D"http=
s://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/scenegraph.html#scenegraph-transformation">T=
ransformations</a></h2><p>A transformation handles object position, rotatio=
n, etc. Its basic property is a dimension count (2D or 3D) and an underlyin=
g numeric type. All classes in <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnu=
m/namespaceMagnum_1_1SceneGraph.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph</a> are te=
mplated on the underlying type. However, in most cases <a href=3D"https://d=
oc.magnum.graphics/magnum/namespaceMagnum.html#a232af7e6fd9b88195fbc59fcec2=
155cb" class=3D"m-doc">Float</a> is used and thus nearly all classes have c=
onvenience aliases so you don't have to explicitly specify it.</p><p>Scene =
graph has various transformation implementations for both 2D and 3D. Each i=
mplementation has its own advantages and disadvantages =E2=80=94 for exampl=
e when using matrices you can have nearly arbitrary transformations, but co=
mposing transformations, calculating their inverse and accounting for float=
ing-point drift is a rather costly operation. On the other hand, quaternion=
s for example won't allow you to scale or shear objects, but have far bette=
r performance characteristics.</p><p>It's also possible to implement your o=
wn transformation class for specific needs, see the source of builtin trans=
formation classes for more information.</p><p>Magnum provides the following=
 transformation classes. See documentation of each class for more detailed =
information:</p><ul><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/class=
Magnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1BasicMatrixTransformation2D.html" class=3D"m-doc">S=
ceneGraph::<wbr>MatrixTransformation2D</a> =E2=80=94 arbitrary 2D transform=
ations but with slow inverse transformations and no floating-point drift re=
duction</li><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1=
_1SceneGraph_1_1BasicMatrixTransformation3D.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGrap=
h::<wbr>MatrixTransformation3D</a> =E2=80=94 arbitrary 3D transformations b=
ut with slow inverse transformations and no floating-point drift reduction<=
/li><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneG=
raph_1_1BasicRigidMatrixTransformation2D.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::=
<wbr>RigidMatrixTransformation2D</a> =E2=80=94 2D translation, rotation and=
 reflection (no scaling), with relatively fast inverse transformations and =
floating-point drift reduction</li><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphi=
cs/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1BasicRigidMatrixTransformation3D.htm=
l" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>RigidMatrixTransformation3D</a> =E2=80=
=94 3D translation, rotation and reflection (no scaling), with relatively f=
ast inverse transformations and floating-point drift reduction</li><li><a h=
ref=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Basi=
cDualComplexTransformation.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>DualCompl=
exTransformation</a> =E2=80=94 2D translation and rotation with fast invers=
e transformations and floating-point drift reduction</li><li><a href=3D"htt=
ps://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1BasicDualQuate=
rnionTransformation.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>DualQuaternionTr=
ansformation</a> =E2=80=94 3D translation and rotation with fast inverse tr=
ansformation and floating-point drift reduction</li><li><a href=3D"https://=
doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1BasicTranslationRot=
ationScalingTransformation2D.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Transla=
tionRotationScalingTransformation2D</a> =E2=80=94 2D transformations with s=
eparate translation, rotation and scaling. Usable for scenes with animation=
 tracks that control these properties separately.</li><li><a href=3D"https:=
//doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1BasicTranslationR=
otationScalingTransformation3D.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Trans=
lationRotationScalingTransformation3D</a> =E2=80=94 3D transformations with=
 separate translation, rotation and scaling. Usable for scenes with animati=
on tracks that control these properties separately.</li><li><a href=3D"http=
s://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1TranslationTran=
sformation.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>TranslationTransformation=
*D</a> =E2=80=94 Just 2D/3D translation (no rotation, scaling or anything e=
lse)</li></ul><p>Common usage of transformation classes is to typedef <a hr=
ef=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Scene=
.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Scene</a> and <a href=3D"https://do=
c.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Object.html" class=3D=
"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Object</a> with desired transformation type to sav=
e unnecessary typing later:</p><pre class=3D"m-code"><span class=3D"k">type=
def</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><spa=
n class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">Scene</span><span class=3D"o">&lt;=
</span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><span class=3D"o">::</span><span =
class=3D"n">MatrixTransformation3D</span><span class=3D"o">&gt;</span><span=
 class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">Scene3D</span><span class=3D"p">;</s=
pan>
<span class=3D"k">typedef</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n"=
>SceneGraph</span><span class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">Object</span=
><span class=3D"o">&lt;</span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><span clas=
s=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">MatrixTransformation3D</span><span class=
=3D"o">&gt;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">;</span></pre><aside class=3D"m-note m-warning"><h4>Att=
ention</h4><p>Note that you have to include both <a href=3D"https://doc.mag=
num.graphics/magnum/src_2Magnum_2SceneGraph_2Object_8h.html" class=3D"m-doc=
">Magnum/<wbr>SceneGraph/<wbr>Object.h</a> and desired transformation class=
 (e.g. <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/MatrixTransformation3D=
_8h.html" class=3D"m-doc">Magnum/<wbr>SceneGraph/<wbr>MatrixTransformation3=
D.h</a>) to be able to use the resulting type.</p></aside><p>The object typ=
e is subclassed from the transformation type and so the <code class=3D"m-co=
de"><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span></code> type will then contain all mem=
bers from both <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_=
1SceneGraph_1_1Object.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Object</a> and=
 <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/namespaceMagnum_1_1SceneGrap=
h.html#ae72540433b3a42ea312c5ec5c70abc6c" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>=
MatrixTransformation3D</a>. For convenience you can use method chaining:</p=
><pre class=3D"m-code"><span class=3D"n">Scene3D</span><span class=3D"w"> <=
/span><span class=3D"n">scene</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>

<span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n=
">object</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"n">object</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">=
setParent</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><sp=
an class=3D"n">scene</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"w">      </span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">=
rotateY</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"mf">15.0</span><span=
 class=3D"n">_degf</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"w">      </span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">=
translate</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">Vector3</span><=
span class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">xAxis</span><span class=3D"p">(=
</span><span class=3D"mf">5.0f</span><span class=3D"p">));</span></pre></se=
ction><section id=3D"scenegraph-hierarchy"><h2><a href=3D"https://doc.magnu=
m.graphics/magnum/scenegraph.html#scenegraph-hierarchy">Scene hierarchy</a>=
</h2><p>Scene hierarchy is an essential part of the scene graph. In the roo=
t there is a <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1S=
ceneGraph_1_1Scene.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Scene</a>, its ch=
ildren are <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1Sce=
neGraph_1_1Object.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Object</a> instanc=
es. The whole hierarchy has a single transformation type, identical for all=
 objects (because for example having part of the tree in 2D and part in 3D =
just wouldn't make sense).</p><p>Build the hierarchy by <em>parenting</em> =
one object to another. Parent object can be either passed in the constructo=
r or set using <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_=
1SceneGraph_1_1Object.html#acdd0a4ffb79b30fab72acfb62a99695a" class=3D"m-do=
c">SceneGraph::<wbr>Object::<wbr>setParent()</a>. The scene is always a roo=
t object, so it naturally cannot have any parent or transformation. Parent =
and children relationships can be observed through <a href=3D"https://doc.m=
agnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Object.html#a2fca1eacd22=
43f94a8af53ad72fadafa" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Object::<wbr>parent=
()</a> and <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1Sce=
neGraph_1_1Object.html#a7f66683bb5de999350c10c31c08e4879" class=3D"m-doc">S=
ceneGraph::<wbr>Object::<wbr>children()</a>.</p><pre class=3D"m-code"><span=
 class=3D"n">Scene3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">scen=
e</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>

<span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"w=
"> </span><span class=3D"n">first</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span cla=
ss=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"k">new</span><s=
pan class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"p">=
{</span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"n">scene</span><span c=
lass=3D"p">};</span>
<span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"w=
"> </span><span class=3D"n">second</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span cl=
ass=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"k">new</span><=
span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"p"=
>{</span><span class=3D"n">first</span><span class=3D"p">};</span></pre><p>=
This hierarchy also takes care of memory management =E2=80=94 when an objec=
t is destroyed, all its children are destroyed too. See detailed explanatio=
n of <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/scenegraph.html#scenegra=
ph-object-construction-order" class=3D"m-doc">construction and destruction =
order</a> below for information about possible issues. To reflect the impli=
cit memory management in the code better, you can use <a href=3D"https://do=
c.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Object.html#a89445e01=
a94a4b43c42b597ab8a84a45" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Object::<wbr>add=
Child()</a> instead of the naked <code class=3D"m-code"><span class=3D"k">n=
ew</span></code> call from the code above:</p><pre class=3D"m-code"><span c=
lass=3D"n">Scene3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">scene<=
/span><span class=3D"p">;</span>

<span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">first</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><spa=
n class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">scene</=
span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">addChild</span><span class=
=3D"o">&lt;</span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"o">&gt;</=
span><span class=3D"p">();</span>
<span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">second</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><sp=
an class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">first<=
/span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">addChild</span><span clas=
s=3D"o">&lt;</span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"o">&gt;<=
/span><span class=3D"p">();</span></pre></section><section id=3D"scenegraph=
-features"><h2><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/scenegraph.htm=
l#scenegraph-features">Object features</a></h2><p>Magnum provides the follo=
wing builtin features. See documentation of each class for more detailed in=
formation and usage examples:</p><ul><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.grap=
hics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Camera.html" class=3D"m-doc">Scene=
Graph::<wbr>Camera*D</a> =E2=80=94 Handles projection matrix, aspect ratio =
correction etc.. Used for rendering parts of the scene.</li><li><a href=3D"=
https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Drawable.ht=
ml" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Drawable*D</a> =E2=80=94 Adds drawing =
functionality to given object. Group of drawables can be then rendered usin=
g the camera feature.</li><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum=
/classMagnum_1_1Audio_1_1Listener.html" class=3D"m-doc">Audio::<wbr>Listene=
r*D</a> =E2=80=94 Handles audio listener properties like position and orien=
tation. Audio equivalent of a camera.</li><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum=
.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1Audio_1_1Playable.html" class=3D"m-doc">Aud=
io::<wbr>Playable*D</a> =E2=80=94 Handles audio source properties. Audio eq=
uivalent of a drawable.</li><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magn=
um/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Animable.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::=
<wbr>Animable*D</a> =E2=80=94 Adds animation functionality to given object.=
 Group of animables can be then controlled using <a href=3D"https://doc.mag=
num.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AnimableGroup.html" class=
=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>AnimableGroup*D</a>.</li><li><a href=3D"https:/=
/doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1DebugTools_1_1ObjectRenderer.htm=
l" class=3D"m-doc">DebugTools::<wbr>ObjectRenderer*D</a>, <a href=3D"https:=
//doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1DebugTools_1_1ForceRenderer.htm=
l" class=3D"m-doc">DebugTools::<wbr>ForceRenderer*D</a> =E2=80=94 Visualize=
 object properties, object shape or force vector for debugging purposes. Se=
e <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/debug-tools.html" class=3D"=
m-doc">Debugging helpers</a> for more information.</li></ul><p>Each feature=
 takes a reference to <em>holder object</em> in its constructor, so adding =
a feature to an object might look just like the following, as in some cases=
 you don't even need to keep the pointer to it. List of object features is =
accessible through <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnu=
m_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractObject.html#aa663eab19821dcb91e48d2f09eb4661f" c=
lass=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Object::<wbr>features()</a>.</p><pre class=
=3D"m-code"><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><spa=
n class=3D"n">o</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"k">new</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">MyF=
eature</span><span class=3D"p">{</span><span class=3D"n">o</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">,</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">some</span><spa=
n class=3D"p">,</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">params</s=
pan><span class=3D"p">};</span></pre><p>Some features are passive, others a=
ctive. Passive features can just be added to an object, with no additional =
work except for possible configuration (for example a debug renderer). Acti=
ve features require the user to implement some virtual function (for exampl=
e to draw the object on screen or perform an animation step). To make thing=
s convenient, features can be added directly to object itself using multipl=
e inheritance, so you can add all the active features you want and implemen=
t functions you need in your own <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/mag=
num/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Object.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<=
wbr>Object</a> subclass without having to subclass each feature individuall=
y (and making the code overly verbose). A simplified example:</p><pre class=
=3D"m-code"><span class=3D"k">class</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span c=
lass=3D"nc">BouncingBall</span><span class=3D"o">:</span><span class=3D"w">=
 </span><span class=3D"k">public</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span clas=
s=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"p">,</span><span class=3D"w"> </span>=
<span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><span class=3D"o">::</span><span class=
=3D"n">Drawable3D</span><span class=3D"p">,</span><span class=3D"w"> </span=
><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><span class=3D"o">::</span><span class=
=3D"n">Animable3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">{</span=
>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">public</span><span class=3D"=
o">:</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"k">explicit</span><span cla=
ss=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">BouncingBall</span><span class=3D"p">(</=
span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"o">*</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">parent</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><sp=
an class=3D"o">:</span>
<span class=3D"w">            </span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span=
 class=3D"p">{</span><span class=3D"n">parent</span><span class=3D"p">},</s=
pan><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><span clas=
s=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">Drawable3D</span><span class=3D"p">{</sp=
an><span class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"k">this</span><span class=3D"p"=
>},</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><spa=
n class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">Animable3D</span><span class=3D"p"=
>{</span><span class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"k">this</span><span class=
=3D"p">}</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">{}</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">private</span><span class=3D=
"o">:</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"c1">// drawing implementati=
on for Drawable feature</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"kt">void</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">draw</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span=
 class=3D"k">const</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">Matrix=
4</span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"p">,</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><span class=3D"o">::</spa=
n><span class=3D"n">Camera3D</span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"k">override</span>=
<span class=3D"p">;</span>

<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"c1">// animation step for A=
nimable feature</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"kt">void</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nf">animationStep</span><span class=3D"p">(</=
span><span class=3D"n">Float</span><span class=3D"p">,</span><span class=3D=
"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">Float</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span c=
lass=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"k">override</span><span class=3D"p">;</sp=
an>
<span class=3D"p">};</span></pre><p>From the outside there is no difference=
 between features added "at runtime" and features added using multiple inhe=
ritance, they can be both accessed from the feature list.</p><p>Similarly t=
o object hierarchy, when destroying object, all its features (both member a=
nd inherited) are destroyed. See detailed explanation of <a href=3D"https:/=
/doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/scenegraph.html#scenegraph-feature-construction=
-order" class=3D"m-doc">construction and destruction order</a> for informat=
ion about possible issues. Also, there is the <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum=
.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractObject.html#a1b700add=
b5404a8f121add73dbde0240" class=3D"m-doc">addFeature()</a> counterpart to <=
a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1O=
bject.html#a89445e01a94a4b43c42b597ab8a84a45" class=3D"m-doc">addChild()</a=
>:</p><pre class=3D"m-code"><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D=
"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">o</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"n">o</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">addFe=
ature</span><span class=3D"o">&lt;</span><span class=3D"n">MyFeature</span>=
<span class=3D"o">&gt;</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">so=
me</span><span class=3D"p">,</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D=
"n">params</span><span class=3D"p">);</span></pre><section id=3D"scenegraph=
-features-caching"><h3><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/sceneg=
raph.html#scenegraph-features-caching">Transformation caching in features</=
a></h3><p>Some features need to operate with absolute transformations and t=
heir inversions =E2=80=94 for example the camera needs its inverse transfor=
mation (camera matrix) to render the scene, collision detection needs to kn=
ow about positions of surrounding objects etc. To avoid computing the trans=
formations from scratch every time, the feature can cache them.</p><p>The c=
ached data stay until the object is marked as <em>dirty</em> =E2=80=94 that=
 is by changing its transformation, its parent or by explicitly calling <a =
href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Obj=
ect.html#a58e943fd6339f2bc9b5016c5cbb5bae3" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wb=
r>Object::<wbr>setDirty()</a>. If the object is marked as dirty, all its ch=
ildren are marked as dirty as well and <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphi=
cs/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractFeature.html#af0c9721f8f2614=
db3364575d58695263" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>AbstractFeature::<wbr>=
markDirty()</a> is called on every feature attached to them. Calling <a hre=
f=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Object=
.html#a553758bb91a137261a2058b773c1095b" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>O=
bject::<wbr>setClean()</a> cleans the dirty object and all its dirty parent=
s =E2=80=94 it goes through all object features and calls <a href=3D"https:=
//doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractFeature.h=
tml#ac7f9f08cd976bc728589cc5eb607b814" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Abs=
tractFeature::<wbr>clean()</a> or <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/ma=
gnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractFeature.html#a38a4e3c18ff8a2636a5=
33af17f6f79db" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>AbstractFeature::<wbr>clean=
Inverted()</a> depending on which caching is enabled on given feature. If t=
he object is already clean, <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/c=
lassMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Object.html#a553758bb91a137261a2058b773c1095b" =
class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Object::<wbr>setClean()</a> does nothing.<=
/p><p>Usually you will need caching in the <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.gr=
aphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Object.html" class=3D"m-doc">Sce=
neGraph::<wbr>Object</a> itself =E2=80=94 which doesn't support it on its o=
wn =E2=80=94 however you can take advantage of multiple inheritance and imp=
lement it using <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1=
_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractFeature.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Abst=
ractFeature</a>. In order to have caching, you must enable it first, becaus=
e by default caching is disabled. You can enable it using <a href=3D"https:=
//doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractFeature.h=
tml#a4b357ec06a582d0eef270eca88134a33" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Abs=
tractFeature::<wbr>setCachedTransformations()</a> and then implement the co=
rresponding cleaning function(s):</p><pre class=3D"m-code"><span class=3D"k=
">class</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nc">CachingObject</s=
pan><span class=3D"o">:</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"k">p=
ublic</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><spa=
n class=3D"p">,</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">SceneGrap=
h</span><span class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">AbstractFeature3D</spa=
n><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">public</span><span class=3D"=
o">:</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"k">explicit</span><span cla=
ss=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">CachingObject</span><span class=3D"p">(<=
/span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"o">*</span><span clas=
s=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">parent</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><s=
pan class=3D"o">:</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">Object3=
D</span><span class=3D"p">{</span><span class=3D"n">parent</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">},</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</sp=
an><span class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">AbstractFeature3D</span><sp=
an class=3D"p">{</span><span class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"k">this</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">}</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">{<=
/span>
<span class=3D"w">            </span><span class=3D"n">setCachedTransformat=
ions</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><sp=
an class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">CachedTransformation</span><span =
class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">Absolute</span><span class=3D"p">);<=
/span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"p">}</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">protected</span><span class=
=3D"o">:</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"kt">void</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">clean</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><spa=
n class=3D"k">const</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">Matri=
x4</span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span clas=
s=3D"n">absoluteTransformation</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"k">override</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><=
span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"w">            </span><span class=3D"n">_absolutePosition</s=
pan><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"=
> </span><span class=3D"n">absoluteTransformation</span><span class=3D"p">.=
</span><span class=3D"n">translation</span><span class=3D"p">();</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"p">}</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">private</span><span class=3D=
"o">:</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"n">Vector3</span><span clas=
s=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">_absolutePosition</span><span class=3D"p"=
>;</span>
<span class=3D"p">};</span></pre><p>When you need to use the cached value, =
you can explicitly request the cleanup by calling <a href=3D"https://doc.ma=
gnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Object.html#a553758bb91a1=
37261a2058b773c1095b" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Object::<wbr>setClea=
n()</a>. <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1Scene=
Graph_1_1Camera.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Camera</a>, for exam=
ple, calls it automatically before it starts rendering, as it needs up-to-d=
ate <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph=
_1_1Camera.html#aae8dcaa154588aeb050b5c865c8e7ae0" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGra=
ph::<wbr>Camera::<wbr>cameraMatrix()</a> to properly draw all objects.</p><=
/section><section id=3D"scenegraph-features-transformation"><h3><a href=3D"=
https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/scenegraph.html#scenegraph-features-tran=
sformation">Polymorphic access to object transformation</a></h3><p>Features=
 by default have access only to <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magn=
um/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractObject.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneG=
raph::<wbr>AbstractObject</a>, which doesn't know about any particular tran=
sformation implementation. This has the advantage that features don't have =
to be implemented for all possible transformation implementations. But, as =
a consequence, it is impossible to transform the object using only a pointe=
r to <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGrap=
h_1_1AbstractObject.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>AbstractObject</=
a>.</p><p>To solve this, the transformation classes are subclassed from int=
erfaces sharing common functionality, so the feature can use that interface=
 instead of being specialized for all relevant transformation implementatio=
ns. The following interfaces are available, each having its own set of virt=
ual functions to control the transformation:</p><ul><li><a href=3D"https://=
doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractTransformat=
ion.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>AbstractTransformation*D</a> =E2=
=80=94 base for all transformations</li><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.g=
raphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractTranslation.html" class=
=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>AbstractTranslation*D</a> =E2=80=94 base for al=
l transformations providing translation</li><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magn=
um.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractBasicTranslationRot=
ation2D.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>AbstractTranslationRotation2=
D</a>, <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGr=
aph_1_1AbstractBasicTranslationRotation3D.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph:=
:<wbr>AbstractTranslationRotation3D</a> =E2=80=94 base for all transformati=
ons providing translation and rotation</li><li><a href=3D"https://doc.magnu=
m.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractBasicTranslationRota=
tionScaling2D.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>AbstractBasicTranslati=
onRotationScaling2D</a>, <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/clas=
sMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractBasicTranslationRotationScaling3D.html" cl=
ass=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>AbstractBasicTranslationRotationScaling3D</a=
> =E2=80=94 base for all transformations providing translation, rotation an=
d scaling</li></ul><p>These interfaces provide virtual functions which can =
be used to modify object transformations. The virtual calls are used only w=
hen calling through the interface and not when using the concrete implement=
ation directly to avoid negative performance effects. There are no function=
s to retrieve object transformation, you need to use the above transformati=
on caching mechanism for that.</p><p>In the following example we are able t=
o get pointer to both the <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/cla=
ssMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractObject.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::=
<wbr>AbstractObject</a> and the needed transformation from a single constru=
ctor parameter using a trick:</p><pre class=3D"m-code"><span class=3D"k">cl=
ass</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nc">TransformingFeature<=
/span><span class=3D"o">:</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"k"=
>public</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span>=
<span class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">AbstractFeature3D</span><span =
class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">public</span><span class=3D"=
o">:</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"k">template</span><span cla=
ss=3D"o">&lt;</span><span class=3D"k">class</span><span class=3D"w"> </span=
><span class=3D"nc">T</span><span class=3D"o">&gt;</span><span class=3D"w">=
 </span><span class=3D"k">explicit</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span cl=
ass=3D"n">TransformingFeature</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><span class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">Object=
</span><span class=3D"o">&lt;</span><span class=3D"n">T</span><span class=
=3D"o">&gt;&amp;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">object</=
span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"o">:</span>
<span class=3D"w">            </span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><sp=
an class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">AbstractFeature3D</span><span cla=
ss=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">object</span><span class=3D"p">),</span>=
<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">_transformation</span><span cla=
ss=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">object</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><=
span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">{}</span>

<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"err">=E2=80=A6</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">private</span><span class=3D=
"o">:</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"n">SceneGraph</span><span c=
lass=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">AbstractTranslationRotation3D</span><=
span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">_t=
ransformation</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"p">};</span></pre><p>If we take for example <a href=3D"https=
://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1Object.html" cla=
ss=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Object&lt;MatrixTransformation3D&gt;</a>, it =
is derived from <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1=
_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractObject.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>Abstr=
actObject3D</a> and <a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagn=
um_1_1SceneGraph_1_1BasicMatrixTransformation3D.html" class=3D"m-doc">Scene=
Graph::<wbr>MatrixTransformation3D</a>. Thus the reference to <a href=3D"ht=
tps://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/classMagnum_1_1SceneGraph_1_1AbstractBasic=
TranslationRotation3D.html" class=3D"m-doc">SceneGraph::<wbr>AbstractTransl=
ationRotation3D</a>, is automatically extracted from the reference in our c=
onstructor.</p></section></section><section id=3D"scenegraph-construction-o=
rder"><h2><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/scenegraph.html#sce=
negraph-construction-order">Construction and destruction order</a></h2><p>T=
here aren't any limitations and usage trade-offs of what you can and can't =
do when working with objects and features, but there are two issues which y=
ou should be aware of.</p><section id=3D"scenegraph-object-construction-ord=
er"><h3><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.graphics/magnum/scenegraph.html#scene=
graph-object-construction-order">Object hierarchy</a></h3><p>When objects a=
re created on the heap (the preferred way, using <code class=3D"m-code"><sp=
an class=3D"k">new</span></code>), they can be constructed in any order and=
 they will be destroyed when their parent is destroyed. When creating them =
on the stack, however, they will be destroyed when they go out of scope. No=
rmally, the natural order of creation is not a problem:</p><pre class=3D"m-=
code"><span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"n">Scene3D</span><span class=3D=
"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">scene</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nf">object</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><s=
pan class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"n">scene</span><span class=3D"p"=
>);</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span></pre><p>The <code>object</code> is created last,=
 so it will be destroyed first, removing itself from <code>scene</code>'s c=
hildren list, causing no problems when destroying the <code>scene</code> ob=
ject later. However, if their order is swapped, it will cause problems:</p>=
<pre class=3D"m-code"><span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">object</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"n">Scene3D</span><span class=3D=
"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">scene</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"n">object</span><span class=3D"=
p">.</span><span class=3D"n">setParent</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><spa=
n class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"n">scene</span><span class=3D"p">)=
;</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"c1">// c=
rash!</span></pre><p>The <code>scene</code> will be destroyed first, deleti=
ng all its children, which is wrong, because <code>object</code> is created=
 on stack. If this doesn't already crash, the <code>object</code> destructo=
r is called (again), making things even worse.</p></section><section id=3D"=
scenegraph-feature-construction-order"><h3><a href=3D"https://doc.magnum.gr=
aphics/magnum/scenegraph.html#scenegraph-feature-construction-order">Member=
 and inherited features</a></h3><p>When destroying the object, all its feat=
ures are destroyed. For features added as a member it's not an issue, howev=
er features added using multiple inheritance must be inherited after the Ob=
ject class:</p><pre class=3D"m-code"><span class=3D"k">class</span><span cl=
ass=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nc">MyObject</span><span class=3D"o">:</sp=
an><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"k">public</span><span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"p">,</span><span=
 class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">MyFeature</span><span class=3D"w"> <=
/span><span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">public</span><span class=3D"=
o">:</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"n">MyObject</span><span cla=
ss=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"o">*</span=
><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">parent</span><span class=3D"p"=
>)</span><span class=3D"o">:</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D=
"n">Object3D</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">parent</span=
><span class=3D"p">),</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">MyF=
eature</span><span class=3D"p">{</span><span class=3D"o">*</span><span clas=
s=3D"k">this</span><span class=3D"p">}</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><spa=
n class=3D"p">{}</span>
<span class=3D"p">};</span></pre><p>When constructing <code>MyObject</code>=
, <code>Object3D</code> constructor is called first and then <code>MyFeatur=
e</code> constructor adds itself to <code>Object3D</code>'s list of feature=
s. When destroying <code>MyObject</code>, its destructor is called and then=
 the destructors of ancestor classes =E2=80=94 first <code>MyFeature</code>=
 destructor, which will remove itself from <code>Object3D</code>'s list, th=
en <code>Object3D</code> destructor.</p><p>However, if we would inherit <co=
de>MyFeature</code> first, it will cause problems:</p><pre class=3D"m-code"=
><span class=3D"k">class</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nc"=
>MyObject</span><span class=3D"o">:</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span c=
